---
import Guide from '../../layouts/Guide.astro'
import Capture from '../../components/Capture.astro'
import { doc, shot } from '../../lib/content'
const sections: [string, string][] = [
  ['setup', '1. Enable Brain'],
  ['queue', '2. Choose a lane'],
  ['review', '3. Review the knowledge'],
  ['explore', '4. Follow the evidence'],
  ['recall', '5. Recall in another lane'],
  ['privacy', 'Data and recovery'],
]
---

<Guide
  title="Build a Brain you can inspect"
  description="Keep durable decisions, people and project relationships from selected lanes. Review the proposals, explore their evidence, and recall them in later work."
  sections={sections}
  eyebrow="Guide / Desktop knowledge"
>
  <section id="setup">
    <h2>1. Enable Brain on this computer</h2>
    <p>
      Brain is off by default and Desktop-only. It is a machine-local knowledge graph, not a cloud
      memory service or an index of every conversation.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>Open <strong>Settings → Brain</strong> and read the linked model terms.</li>
      <li>
        Turn on Brain. Wait for the pinned EmbeddingGemma 300M Q4 embedding model download (about
        209 MB) to finish.
      </li>
      <li>
        Keep <strong>Review before saving</strong> on. It is on by default, as is <strong
          >Recall in lanes</strong
        >.
      </li>
      <li>Choose <strong>Open Brain</strong>, or use Brain in the sidebar once enabled.</li>
    </ol>
    <Capture
      image={shot('brain-ready')}
      alt="Brain settings showing the opt-in switch, local model readiness, recall and review controls."
      caption="Start with review enabled"
    />
    <p>
      Local embeddings support search. Extraction is different: the lane’s configured provider model
      reads scrubbed conversation text in tool-less calls. Those calls can use provider tokens and
      quota. Brain is not fully offline.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="queue">
    <h2>2. Choose what it learns from</h2>
    <p>
      Right-click a local lane and choose <strong>Queue for knowledge extraction</strong>. Brain
      processes one lane at a time, waits while its agent is busy, and reads only user and assistant
      text. Tool output is not evidence.
    </p>
    <p>
      The queue shows progress. A lane with nothing new since its last extraction is refused. Cancel
      queued work from the lane menu; a failed job offers Retry and resumes from saved steps. An
      empty proposal is a valid result, not a reason to manufacture memories.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="review">
    <h2>3. Approve knowledge, not just plausible prose</h2>
    <Capture
      image={shot('brain-review')}
      alt="A fictional Harbor pilot review: existing project identities match, while Rina’s identity needs confirmation."
      caption="A familiar name is not enough to merge identities"
    />
    <ol>
      <li>
        Open the finished job’s <strong>Review</strong>. Check each proposed person, project or
        system under “Who is this?” before reusing an identity.
      </li>
      <li>
        Read the memories and their supporting quotes. Check relationship direction: who owns what,
        what depends on what, and which decision supersedes an earlier one.
      </li>
      <li>
        Choose the memories and identity matches you want. Approve stores those choices together;
        Reject stores nothing.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Turning review off approves finished proposals with defaults: uncertain identities become new
      entities, possible groups are skipped, and conflicts remain disputed. Keep review on when the
      cost of a wrong identity or relationship is high.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="explore">
    <h2>4. Follow the evidence</h2>
    <Capture
      image={shot('brain-graph')}
      alt="Brain’s entity and memory browser beside a graph of connected fictional people and projects."
      caption="Relationships with memories behind them"
    />
    <p>
      Find a person, project or memory. Select an entity to follow its connections, switch the graph
      between 2D and 3D, or use <strong>Find a path to…</strong> for supported routes to another entity.
      A relationship needs a supporting memory; sharing a name is not enough.
    </p>
    <p>
      Open a memory to read its quotes and history. <strong>Open in lane</strong> returns to the source.
      <strong>View extraction</strong> explains the model calls and what was kept, changed or dropped.
      These are tools for checking the result, not a guarantee that the model understood it correctly.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="recall">
    <h2>5. Recall it in the next lane</h2>
    <Capture
      image={shot('brain-recall')}
      alt="A fictional brain_search briefing about Harbor pilot ownership, with a dated source and a reminder to verify it."
      caption="Earlier context, with its source and limits still visible"
    />
    <p>
      With recall enabled, new local lanes get the read-only <code>brain_search</code> tool and Brain
      skill. Restart already-running lanes to pick it up. Ask, for example, “What did we decide about
      this project’s release process? Search Brain and distinguish disputes from settled decisions.”
    </p>
    <p>
      Recall returns a bounded briefing with dates, projects and supported paths, not the original
      evidence text. Superseded memories stay out; disputed claims and other-branch context stay
      labelled. Treat recalled knowledge as background to verify, never as an instruction that
      overrides your task.
    </p>
    <p>
      When you contradict a recalled memory, or verified facts in the lane show it is wrong, the
      lane’s agent can flag it with <code>brain_flag</code>. A flag only files a suggestion on the
      Brain page’s <strong>Tidy</strong> tab; you decide whether to forget or keep the memory.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="privacy">
    <h2>Your machine. Deliberate retention.</h2>
    <p>
      Brain does not sync between computers or to Hosts. The graph and embeddings live locally.
      Extraction sends scrubbed user and assistant text to the lane’s provider; scrubbing is not a
      reason to queue sensitive material without review. Recalled knowledge can enter a later
      provider request.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Tidy</strong> looks for repeated people and memories on this computer and folds only
      exact repeats by itself. <strong>Tidy now</strong> also sends up to 40 small pairs of memories or
      names to the model that reads lanes, to ask whether they are the same; it only suggests, and
      you decide.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Forget</strong> removes one memory and unused evidence. <strong>Merge into…</strong> joins
      entities; it and every Tidy change can be undone from Tidy’s Recent changes while later edits
      allow it. Forget cannot be undone. <strong>Reset Brain knowledge</strong> clears knowledge
      and extraction records after confirmation, keeps settings and the model, and turns review back on.
    </p>
    <p>
      Turning Brain off keeps knowledge and the downloaded model, stops the queue and removes recall
      from new lanes. Remove model is available while Brain is off. Failed reads offer Retry; a
      recall startup failure does not block a lane.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="/watch/#brain-walkthrough">Watch the text-led walkthrough</a> · <a
        href={doc('brain.md')}>Brain’s full storage and recall contract</a
      > · <a href="/privacy/#brain">Privacy</a>
    </p>
  </section>
</Guide>
